course: dictionary
lesson: 14604
title: Land won back from the water
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A polder is a piece of low, flat land that was once under the sea or a lake. People took it back from the water and keep it dry with walls of earth called dykes. Polders are mainly found in the Netherlands.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Dykes keep the sea out of the polder.» Walls keep water out, so a polder must be land that stays dry because of them. Check it by swapping in your meaning: «Dykes keep the sea out of the flat, dry land that was once sea» still makes sense.
explain: Pumps are another clue. «The windmills pumped water out of the polder.» Water has to be pumped away, or the land would flood again. So a polder needs constant work to stay dry, and it is not just any flat field.
fact: «Polder» means a piece of low, flat land taken back from the sea or a lake.
fact: A polder is protected by dykes, which are walls of earth.
fact: Most polders are in the Netherlands, a country where much of the land is low.
fact: About a quarter of the Netherlands is below sea level, so many polders lie under the level of the water around them.
fact: Long ago, windmills pumped water out of polders; today, pumps do the job.
exercise: «The farmer's cows grazed on the flat green polder.» What is a polder? | A deep, dry cave under the ground | *A piece of low, flat land taken back from the water | A tall hill covered in trees | A kind of boat used on canals
exercise: «Dykes keep the sea out of the polder.» What are dykes? | *Walls of earth that keep water out | Wooden bridges over a canal | Fields of wheat and barley | Small rivers that run to the sea
exercise: Ella says, «Farms and towns stand on the polder.» Find and copy one word that names a kind of land. | towns | farms | stand | *polder
test: «The windmills pumped water out of the polder.» Why do the pumps matter? | They carry visitors across the land | They make the dykes taller | They make the water warmer | *They keep the water out of the land so it stays dry
test: Jayden asks, «Is a polder land that was always dry?» What is the answer? | *No, a polder was once under water and was taken back from it | Yes, polders have always been dry | No, polders are flat land taken back from the sea or a lake | Yes, but only in summer
exam: «About a quarter of the Netherlands is below sea level.» What does polder most likely mean in this lesson? | A high mountain path with steep drops | A town built on a hill above the sea | *Low, flat land won back from the water and kept dry by dykes | A kind of Dutch cheese
